## 3. Packages in the lockfile that are NOT built for Android

`flutter_blue_plus` is a **federated plugin**, so `pubspec.lock` also resolves
its Linux, Darwin and web implementations and *their* dependencies even though
only the Android one is ever compiled. `dbus` and `bluez` arrive this way, as
transitive dependencies of `flutter_blue_plus_linux`.

| Package | Version | Licence | In the APK? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `flutter_blue_plus_linux` | 7.0.3 | BSD-3-Clause | no |
| `flutter_blue_plus_darwin` | 7.0.3 | BSD-3-Clause | no |
| `flutter_blue_plus_web` | 7.0.2 | BSD-3-Clause | no |
| `objective_c` | 9.5.0 | BSD-3-Clause | no |
| `dbus` | 0.7.15 | **MPL-2.0** | **no** |
| `bluez` | 0.8.3 | **MPL-2.0** | **no** |

`dbus` and `bluez` are the only copyleft dependencies in the entire tree.

**They are not in the Android build.** The evidence is that the Android build
registers exactly four plugins
(`app/android/app/src/main/java/io/flutter/plugins/GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java`:
`flutter_blue_plus`, `jni`, `jni_flutter`, `permission_handler`), and neither
`dbus` nor `bluez` is among them; they exist in `pubspec.lock` only because of
how a federated plugin's platform implementations are resolved. They are listed
here so that a reader auditing the lockfile does not have to re-derive that.

Even if they were compiled in, MPL-2.0 is a **file-level** copyleft licence: it
places conditions on modified MPL-2.0 *files*, not on the larger work they are
combined into, and MPL-2.0 §3.3 permits distribution of a Larger Work under any
terms provided the MPL-2.0 files are not marked "Incompatible With Secondary
Licenses". Neither package carries that (Exhibit B) notice. Nothing here is
modified. The licence text is at <https://www.mozilla.org/en-US/MPL/2.0/>.

